WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.490
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:16.366
Unit forty-one: Biotechnology. In this unit, you will debate the promise and dangers of biotechnology.

4
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.595
gene editing.

5
00:00:19.295 --> 00:00:24.568
gene editing means changing the DNA of a living thing in a precise way.

6
00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:27.414
Gene editing may cure inherited diseases.

7
00:00:28.114 --> 00:00:31.764
Your turn. Say: gene editing.

8
00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:35.628
gene editing.

9
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:37.455
CRISPR.

10
00:00:38.155 --> 00:00:41.745
CRISPR means a powerful tool that can cut and edit DNA.

11
00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:45.717
CRISPR has made gene editing faster and cheaper.

12
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:49.394
Your turn. Say: CRISPR.

13
00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:52.909
CRISPR.

14
00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:55.487
synthetic biology.

15
00:00:56.187 --> 00:01:01.646
synthetic biology means designing and building new biological parts or organisms.

16
00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:05.327
Synthetic biology could produce fuel from bacteria.

17
00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:09.422
Your turn. Say: synthetic biology.

18
00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:13.716
synthetic biology.

19
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.356
biotech start-up.

20
00:01:17.056 --> 00:01:22.144
biotech start-up means a new company developing products from biology.

21
00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:26.545
The biotech start-up is developing a malaria test.

22
00:01:27.245 --> 00:01:30.594
Your turn. Say: biotech start-up.

23
00:01:33.294 --> 00:01:35.259
biotech start-up.

24
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.472
designer baby.

25
00:01:38.172 --> 00:01:42.261
designer baby means a baby whose genes have been chosen or altered.

26
00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:45.547
Most scientists oppose creating designer babies.

27
00:01:46.247 --> 00:01:49.736
Your turn. Say: designer baby.

28
00:01:52.436 --> 00:01:53.762
designer baby.

29
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.355
genetic modification.

30
00:01:57.055 --> 00:02:01.179
genetic modification means changing the genes of crops or animals.

31
00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:05.081
Genetic modification has produced drought-resistant rice.

32
00:02:05.781 --> 00:02:09.490
Your turn. Say: genetic modification.

33
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.829
genetic modification.

34
00:02:14.817 --> 00:02:15.887
stem cell.

35
00:02:16.587 --> 00:02:21.292
stem cell means a cell that can develop into many types of cells.

36
00:02:21.592 --> 00:02:24.904
Stem cell research offers hope for spinal injuries.

37
00:02:25.604 --> 00:02:28.453
Your turn. Say: stem cell.

38
00:02:31.153 --> 00:02:32.108
stem cell.

39
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:34.362
biosafety.

40
00:02:35.062 --> 00:02:40.045
biosafety means rules and practices to prevent harm from biological research.

41
00:02:40.345 --> 00:02:43.331
Strict biosafety rules govern the laboratory.

42
00:02:44.031 --> 00:02:47.276
Your turn. Say: biosafety.

43
00:02:49.976 --> 00:02:51.105
biosafety.

44
00:02:52.117 --> 00:02:54.337
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.637 --> 00:03:02.151
Rithy, by the time CRISPR was announced in 2012, scientists had been searching for such a tool for decades.

46
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.226
And within a few years, labs everywhere were using it.

47
00:03:08.517 --> 00:03:16.196
Then, in 2018, a researcher shocked the world. He had secretly edited the genes of twin babies.

48
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:21.134
The so-called designer babies? I remember reading about it.

49
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:27.120
He was going to present it as a medical breakthrough, but scientists condemned it.

50
00:03:28.417 --> 00:03:31.484
Because nobody knew the long-term effects?

51
00:03:32.783 --> 00:03:40.916
Exactly. Gene editing to cure disease is one thing; changing future generations is quite another.

52
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:45.911
So strong biosafety rules must keep pace with the science.

53
00:03:47.217 --> 00:03:50.818
Grammar focus: Narrative tenses (mastery).

54
00:03:51.118 --> 00:03:55.045
Mastering narrative tenses means controlling time and emphasis.

55
00:03:55.345 --> 00:04:00.343
Scientists had been searching for decades. He was going to present it as a breakthrough.

56
00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:03.652
Labs everywhere were using it within a few years.

57
00:04:03.952 --> 00:04:09.876
Mastery means choosing tenses to control the reader's sense of time, background and surprise.

58
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:13.420
Now read and listen. Editing Life.

59
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.283
For most of history, humans could only change living things through slow selective breeding.

60
00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:28.283
In 2012, scientists announced CRISPR, a tool that could edit DNA with remarkable precision.

61
00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:37.144
Since then, gene editing has been used to develop disease-resistant crops and potential cures for blood disorders.

62
00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:44.188
Synthetic biology goes further, designing entirely new biological systems.

63
00:04:45.483 --> 00:04:51.268
However, in 2018 a researcher claimed to have created the first gene-edited babies.

64
00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:57.392
The scientific community reacted with alarm, and he was later imprisoned.

65
00:04:58.683 --> 00:05:04.328
The case showed that biosafety and ethics must develop as quickly as the technology itself.

66
00:05:05.617 --> 00:05:13.758
Biotechnology may transform medicine and agriculture, but it also forces us to ask what kind of future we want.

67
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.795
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:18.095 --> 00:05:21.418
A tool that can cut and edit DNA is blank.

69
00:05:21.718 --> 00:05:26.115
A, CRISPR. B, stem cell. C, biosafety.

70
00:05:30.117 --> 00:05:32.523
The answer is A: CRISPR.

71
00:05:34.017 --> 00:05:38.315
By 2012, scientists blank for such a tool for years.

72
00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:43.070
A, searched. B, had been searching. C, have searched.

73
00:05:47.083 --> 00:05:49.838
The answer is B: had been searching.

74
00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.812
He blank announce the results, but he changed his mind.

75
00:05:55.112 --> 00:05:59.230
A, was going to. B, had. C, would have.

76
00:06:03.217 --> 00:06:05.715
The answer is A: was going to.

77
00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:10.598
A baby whose genes have been chosen or altered is a blank.

78
00:06:10.898 --> 00:06:16.873
A, designer baby. B, stem cell. C, biotech start-up.

79
00:06:20.883 --> 00:06:23.429
The answer is A: designer baby.

80
00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:30.074
According to the text, what happened to the researcher who edited babies' genes?

81
00:06:30.374 --> 00:06:36.675
A, he won a prize. B, he was imprisoned. C, he became famous.

82
00:06:40.683 --> 00:06:43.391
The answer is B: he was imprisoned.

83
00:06:44.883 --> 00:06:50.090
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should gene editing be allowed in humans?

84
00:06:55.390 --> 00:07:00.942
Part two: Narrate one real case, then give your position and two reasons.

85
00:07:07.242 --> 00:07:23.316
In 2018, a scientist edited babies' genes in secret, which shocked the world. I believe editing should be allowed only to treat serious diseases in adults, because the risks to future generations are unknown and consent is impossible.

86
00:07:23.616 --> 00:07:26.114
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:27.117 --> 00:07:32.045
Great work today! You learned eight new words and narrative tenses (mastery).

88
00:07:32.345 --> 00:07:36.994
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:37.294 --> 00:07:40.074
See you in Unit forty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:40.374 --> 00:07:43.421
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

